    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Various 2D models exist of the scaling relationship between fault slip and fault length. Although these models predict simple relationships, field data show considerable variations. Even within one area and rock type, the ratio may vary by about one and a half orders of magnitude. This variability can be fully explained by (i) the notion that faults are bounded 3D, and (ii) the occurrence of mechanical interaction between neighboring faults. Interaction along discontinuous faults that consist of sidestepping segments, also explains why overlapping segments are more common than underlapping segments. The maximum distance that segments can overlap is a few times their separation, but depends amongst other things on the 3D fault shape. The analyses suggest that faults with limited down-dip height can overlap\r\nfor considerable distances. Interaction may also change the fault shape. In the overlap zone between laterally-sidestepping segments,the fault tip lines are expected to become straight and parallel to the slip direction. Between vertically stepping segments, the tip lines tend to become horizontal. The predicted straight tip lines are observed in detailed interpretations of 3D seismic data. The juxtaposition of layers along a fault is more complicated when the fault interacts with other faults, and can become very asymmetric. Juxtaposition becomes even more complicated if the fault is segmented.In such cases, the sealing potential is likely to be severely reduced.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
